Titre$P H O L I O T I N A  -  ARNOLDS in FLORA AG. NERL.  - Clé additionnelle 3
Com$adaptée par Paul Bertéa Octobre 2013
1,0$ KEY 3 : Veil absent. Cheilocystidia subcylindrical, lageniform or utriforrn (sect.Piliferae).
1,0n$N.B.The veilin species of Pholiotina is ephernerous and may be absent in older basidiocarps or underwet conditions. See notes under key one and two. One may try to identfy such specimens with the present key. When the presence of veil is doubt ful it is better to use keyfour, which treats all species of this genus.
1,0$ Retour au Kit $$f"Pholiotina_kit.cle"
1,0$
1,2 $ Basidiocarp with green or blue tinge on pileus and/or stipe
1,3 $ Basidiocarps without green or blue tinge

2,0 $ Pileus greyish brown to reddish brown without blue colours; stipe whitish, at base becoming bluish grey to blue-green when bruised and in age; spores7.0-9.0 x 4.0-5.5 µm 22.	 $ Ph. cyanopus
2,0 $ Pileus blue-green, at least at centre; stipe whitish; spores8.0-11.5 x 4.5-6.5 µm 23. $ Ph. aeruginosa

3,0 $ Pileus strongly plicate-sulcate up to halfway the radius 21. $ Ph. sulcata
3,4 $ Pileus not sulcate, often translucently striate or rugulose near centre

4,0 $ Spores 11.0-14.0 x 6.5-8.0 µm; habitat on dung 20. $ Ph. coprophila
4,5 $ Spores less than 10.5 µm long; habitat on soil

5,6 $ Basidiocarps small: pileus 4-20 mm wide, stipe10-45 x 0.5-2 mm; pileus strongly translucently stiate at least up to half of the radius when moist and fresh
5,10 $ Basidiocarps relatively stout: pileus 15-55 mm wide, stipe 25-75 x 2-7 mm; pileus not translucently striate or weakly striate at margin only

6,0 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly utriform and clavate; spores minutely verruculose under oil-immersion lens, in some collections seemingly smooth (but verruculose under SEM)13. $ Ph. utriformis
6,7 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly lageniform or lanceolate to subcylindrial; spores smooth

7,0 $ Stipe white fibrillose-striate, not pruinose; spores 7.0-9.0 x 4.5-5.5 µm, on the average7.8-8.5 µm long, with slightly eccentric germ pore; pileocystidia absent or scarce and obscure 22.	 $ Ph. cyanopus
7,8 $ Stipe pruinose to minutely pubescent, not fibrillose-striate; spores with central germ pore; pileocystidia numerous and striking

8,0 $ Spores7.5-11.0 x 4.5-6.0 µm, on the average longer than 8.2 µm; clamp-connections present; pileocystidia 37-95 x 8.0-16 µm  19. $ Ph. aberrans
8,9 $ Spores 6.0-9.0 x 3.5-5.5 µm, on the average less than 8.2 µm long; clamp-connections absent; pileocystidia up to45 µm long

9,0 $ Cheilocystidia 17-45 x 5.0-8.0 µm, lageniform to lanceolate, gradually tapering into neck, 1.5-2.0(4.0) µm wide, with acute or subacute apex 17.  $ Ph. mairei
9,0 $ Cheilocystidia 46-66(75) x 9.0-11 µm, subcylindrical to lageniform 18. $ Ph. parvula

10,0 $ Spores on the average 8.8-9.6 µm long  15. $ Ph. pygmaeoaffinis
10,0 $ Spores on the average 7.3-8.0 µm long  16.  $ Ph. striipes
